{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Oropharyngeal cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the oropharynx.","source_span":"Oropharyngeal cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the oropharynx."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"The oropharynx is the middle part of the pharynx (throat), located behind the mouth.","source_span":"The oropharynx is the middle part of the pharynx (throat), behind the mouth."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"The pharynx is a hollow tube about 5 inches long that starts behind the nose and ends where the trachea and esophagus begin.","source_span":"The pharynx is a hollow tube about 5 inches long that starts behind the nose and ends where the trachea (windpipe) and esophagus (tube from the throat to the stomach) begin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"The oropharynx includes the soft palate, the side and back walls of the throat, the tonsils, and the back one-third of the tongue.","source_span":"The oropharynx includes the following: - Soft palate. - Side and back walls of the throat. - Tonsils. - Back one-third of the tongue."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Most oropharyngeal cancers are squamous cell carcinomas.","source_span":"Most oropharyngeal cancers are squamous cell carcinomas."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"A history of smoking cigarettes for more than 10 pack years and other tobacco use is a risk factor for oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"A history of smoking cigarettes for more than 10 pack years and other tobacco use."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Being infected with human papillomavirus (HPV), especially HPV type 16, is a risk factor for oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"Being infected with human papillomavirus (HPV), especially HPV type 16."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Heavy alcohol use is a risk factor for oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"Heavy alcohol use."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Chewing betel quid, a stimulant commonly used in parts of Asia, is a risk factor for oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"Chewing betel quid, a stimulant commonly used in parts of Asia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Signs and symptoms of oropharyngeal cancer include a lump in the neck and a sore throat.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of oropharyngeal cancer include a lump in the neck and a sore throat."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Other symptoms of oropharyngeal cancer include trouble swallowing, trouble opening the mouth fully, trouble moving the tongue, unexplained weight loss, ear pain, and coughing up blood.","source_span":"Trouble swallowing.    - Trouble opening the mouth fully.    - Trouble moving the tongue.    - Weight loss for no known reason.    - Ear pain.  ... - Coughing up blood."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"A PET-CT scan combines pictures from a PET scan and a CT scan, both done at the same time with the same machine, to give more detailed pictures than either scan alone.","source_span":"A procedure that combines the pictures from a positron emission tomography (PET) scan and a computed tomography (CT) scan. The PET and CT scans are done at the same time with the same machine."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"A fine-needle biopsy, using a thin needle, is usually done to remove a tissue sample for diagnosing oropharyngeal cancer.","source_span":"A fine-needle biopsy is usually done to remove a sample of tissue using a thin needle."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","statement":"Laryngoscopy is a procedure in which the doctor checks the larynx.","source_span":"Laryngoscopy : A procedure in which the doctor checks the larynx with a"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is oropharyngeal cancer and where is the oropharynx located?","answer":"Oropharyngeal cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the oropharynx, which is the middle part of the pharynx (throat), located behind the mouth."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What structures make up the oropharynx?","answer":"The oropharynx includes the soft palate, the side and back walls of the throat, the tonsils, and the back one-third of the tongue."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What is the most common type of oropharyngeal cancer?","answer":"Most oropharyngeal cancers are squamous cell carcinomas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","fact_refs":[5,6,7,8],"question":"What are the main risk factors for oropharyngeal cancer?","answer":"Key risk factors include smoking cigarettes for more than 10 pack years and other tobacco use, a personal history of head and neck cancer, heavy alcohol use, HPV infection (especially HPV type 16), and chewing betel quid."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","fact_refs":[9,10],"question":"What symptoms might indicate oropharyngeal cancer?","answer":"Symptoms can include a lump in the neck, a sore throat that doesn't go away, trouble swallowing, trouble opening the mouth fully or moving the tongue, unexplained weight loss, ear pain, and coughing up blood."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_6","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What is a PET-CT scan and why is it used in diagnosing oropharyngeal cancer?","answer":"A PET-CT scan combines pictures from a PET scan and a CT scan taken at the same time with the same machine, giving more detailed pictures inside the body than either scan alone."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"Paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity.","source_span":"Paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"The frontal sinuses are located in the lower forehead above the nose.","source_span":"The frontal sinuses are in the lower forehead above the nose."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"The maxillary sinuses are located in the cheekbones on either side of the nose.","source_span":"The maxillary sinuses are in the cheekbones on either side of the nose."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"The ethmoid sinuses are located beside the upper nose, between the eyes.","source_span":"The ethmoid sinuses are beside the upper nose, between the eyes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"The sphenoid sinuses are located behind the nose, in the center of the skull.","source_span":"The sphenoid sinuses are behind the nose, in the center of the skull."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"The most common type of paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer is squamous cell carcinoma.","source_span":"The most common type of paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer is squamous cell carcinoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"Being exposed to certain workplace chemicals or dust, such as in furniture-making, sawmill work, woodworking, shoemaking, metal-plating, or flour mill/bakery work, is a risk factor for paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer.","source_span":"Being exposed to certain workplace chemicals or dust, such as those found in the following jobs: - Furniture-making.- Sawmill work.- Woodworking (carpentry).- Shoemaking.- Metal-plating.- Flour mill or bakery work."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"Being male and older than 40 years is a risk factor for paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer.","source_span":"Being male and older than 40 years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"Signs of paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer include sinus problems and nosebleeds.","source_span":"Signs of paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer include sinus problems and nosebleeds."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"There are three types of biopsy used to diagnose paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer: fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy, incisional biopsy, and excisional biopsy.","source_span":"There are three types of biopsy: - Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy : The removal of tissue or fluid using a thin needle.- Incisional biopsy... - Excisional biopsy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","statement":"Inverting papilloma is a benign tumor that forms inside the nose, and a small number of these change into cancer.","source_span":"Inverting papilloma: Benign tumors that form inside the nose. A small number of these change into cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer?","answer":"It is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","fact_refs":[1,2,3,4],"question":"Where are the four paranasal sinuses located?","answer":"The frontal sinuses are in the lower forehead above the nose, the maxillary sinuses are in the cheekbones on either side of the nose, the ethmoid sinuses are beside the upper nose between the eyes, and the sphenoid sinuses are behind the nose in the center of the skull."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is the most common type of paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer?","answer":"The most common type is squamous cell carcinoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"What occupational and demographic risk factors are linked to paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer?","answer":"Risk factors include exposure to workplace chemicals or dust in jobs like furniture-making, sawmill work, woodworking, shoemaking, metal-plating, and flour mill or bakery work, as well as being male and older than 40 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What are common signs of paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer?","answer":"Common signs include sinus problems and nosebleeds."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_7","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What are the three types of biopsy used to diagnose paranasal sinus and nasal cavity cancer?","answer":"The three types are fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy, incisional biopsy, and excisional biopsy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"Salivary gland cancer is a rare disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the salivary glands.","source_span":"Salivary gland cancer is a rare disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the salivary glands."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"There are 3 pairs of major salivary glands: parotid glands, sublingual glands, and submandibular glands.","source_span":"There are 3 pairs of major salivary glands: - Parotid glands... - Sublingual glands... - Submandibular glands"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"The parotid glands are the largest salivary glands, found in front of and just below each ear, and most major salivary gland tumors begin in this gland.","source_span":"Parotid glands: These are the largest salivary glands and are found in front of and just below each ear. Most major salivary gland tumors begin in this gland."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"More than half of all salivary gland tumors are benign (not cancerous) and do not spread to other tissues.","source_span":"More than half of all salivary gland tumors are benign (not cancerous) and do not spread to other tissues."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"Risk factors for salivary gland cancer include older age, treatment with radiation therapy to the head and neck, and being exposed to certain substances at work.","source_span":"risk factors include the following: - Older age.    - Treatment with radiation therapy to the head and neck.    - Being exposed to certain substances at work."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"Signs of salivary gland cancer include a lump or trouble swallowing.","source_span":"Signs of salivary gland cancer include a lump or trouble swallowing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"Fine needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy is the most common type of biopsy used for salivary gland cancer.","source_span":"An FNA is the most common type of biopsy used for salivary gland cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"The stages used for major salivary gland cancers are Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV.","source_span":"The following stages are used for major salivary gland cancers: - Stage I - Stage II - Stage III - Stage IV"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","statement":"Most small (minor) salivary gland tumors begin in the palate (roof of the mouth).","source_span":"Most small salivary gland tumors begin in the palate (roof of the mouth)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the three pairs of major salivary glands?","answer":"The three pairs of major salivary glands are the parotid glands, sublingual glands, and submandibular glands."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which salivary gland is the largest, where is it located, and where do most major salivary gland tumors begin?","answer":"The parotid glands are the largest salivary glands, located in front of and just below each ear, and most major salivary gland tumors begin in this gland."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What proportion of salivary gland tumors are benign?","answer":"More than half of all salivary gland tumors are benign (not cancerous) and do not spread to other tissues."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the risk factors for salivary gland cancer?","answer":"Risk factors include older age, treatment with radiation therapy to the head and neck, and exposure to certain substances at work."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the most common type of biopsy used to diagnose salivary gland cancer?","answer":"Fine needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy is the most common type of biopsy used for salivary gland cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000024_8","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What stages are used to classify major salivary gland cancers?","answer":"Major salivary gland cancers are classified into Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV."}
